    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d267">

                <pb facs="0791" n="775" sameAs="frus:frus1950v01/pg775"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">International Trade Files, Lot 57D284, Box 165,
                    Folder “Balance of Payments”</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chairman of the United States Delegation to the
                        Fifth Session of the Contracting Parties to GATT
                    </hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Brown</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Acting Director of the Office of International
                        Trade Policy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Leddy</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">personal and confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Torquay</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1950-11-25">November 25, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear John</hi>: Supplementing my letter of November
                    23 about the British attitude on this consultation I heard from John Deutsch
                    yesterday that Dana Wilgress<anchor n="1" xml:id="d267-f1"/> had seen both Sir
                    John Henry Wood<anchor n="2" xml:id="d267-f2"/> and Sir Wilson Smith,<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d267-f3"/> and that both of them had expressed satisfaction at
                    the atmosphere which had prevailed in the consultation committee. He pointed out
                    that these two are the leaders of the “co-operate with North America” school of
                    thought in the British Government, but since they are the two top civil servants
                    in their respective departments it is nice to know that they feel the
                    atmosphere, on our part at least, has been friendly.</p>
                <pb facs="0792" n="776" xml:id="pg776"/>
                <p>I am going, to London next week to talk about you know what, and hope to see both
                    Roger Makins<anchor n="4" xml:id="d267-f4"/> and Gaitskell,<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d267-f5"/> if it can be done informally and if the Embassy agrees,
                    and just tell them the general attitude with which we have approached the
                    problem here.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winthrop</hi> G. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Brown</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d267-f1" n="1" xml:id="d267-f1-content">
                <p>L. Dana Wilgress, High Commissioner for Canada in the United Kingdom, was
                    Chairman of the Contracting Parties.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d267-f2" n="2" xml:id="d267-f2-content">
                <p>Permanent Secretary of the British Board of Trade.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d267-f3" n="3" xml:id="d267-f3-content">
                <p>Sir Henry Wilson Smith (Wilson Smith) was Permanent Secretary of the British
                    Ministry of Defence.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d267-f4" n="4" xml:id="d267-f4-content">
                <p>Sir Roger Makins was a Deputy Under Secretary of State, Foreign Office.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d267-f5" n="5" xml:id="d267-f5-content">
                <p>Hugh T. N. Gaitskell, British Chancellor of the Exchequer.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
